WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.144 --> 00:00:07.924 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Welcome to Asia episode four of seven. First, I clear the chosen names for a
fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:08.576 --> 00:00:26.504 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Six inland countries connect the steppe with high mountains: Kazakhstan,
Uzbekistan, Turkmenistan, Kyrgyzstan, Tajikistan and Afghanistan. Russia lies
to the north; it was already a full stop in our Europe series.

3
00:00:27.185 --> 00:00:31.110 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Kazakhstan and its neighbours into view.

4
00:00:35.249 --> 00:00:47.707 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kazakhstan is the huge northern anchor of Central Asia, below Russia and west
of China. Its western edge reaches the Caspian, which is an inland sea rather
than an ocean outlet.

5
00:00:48.368 --> 00:00:59.757 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Saryarka wetlands are important stopovers for migrating water birds. These
lakes link breeding grounds in Siberia with routes toward Europe, Africa and
southern Asia.

6
00:01:00.413 --> 00:01:11.036 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture migrating birds crossing a very broad steppe country. Kazakhstan caps
the Central Asian group; the smaller countries cluster beneath it.

7
00:01:12.448 --> 00:01:16.326 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Uzbekistan and its neighbours into view.

8
00:01:21.613 --> 00:01:32.701 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Uzbekistan sits below Kazakhstan and between Turkmenistan, Tajikistan and
Kyrgyzstan. A short southern border touches Afghanistan; it has no coastline.

9
00:01:33.354 --> 00:01:43.292 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Samarkand’s historic monuments include Ulugh Beg’s observatory. A Silk Road
city was also a place to study the sky, connecting trade with astronomy.

10
00:01:43.957 --> 00:01:55.555 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Uzbekistan in the central cluster, with Samarkand’s observatory as
its hook. The Caspian coast belongs to Turkmenistan and Kazakhstan, not
Uzbekistan.

11
00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:07.854 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Turkmenistan lies east of the Caspian Sea, below Uzbekistan and above Iran.
Afghanistan touches its southeastern side.

12
00:02:08.508 --> 00:02:19.038 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Ancient Merv was an oasis city on the Silk Roads. Its remains span thousands
of years, showing how a dependable source of water could sustain a major
desert crossroads.

13
00:02:19.704 --> 00:02:29.549 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture an oasis east of the Caspian. Turkmenistan is the southwestern member
of our Central Asian group, between that inland sea and Afghanistan.

14
00:02:31.875 --> 00:02:40.791 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kyrgyzstan is the mountainous country below Kazakhstan, east of Uzbekistan and
north of Tajikistan. China borders its eastern side.

15
00:02:41.448 --> 00:02:55.647 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Manas epic belongs to a living tradition of storytelling. Specialist
narrators perform long accounts of heroes, carrying historical memory and
community values through spoken words.

16
00:02:56.313 --> 00:03:06.762 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember the storyteller in the northern mountain pocket beside China.
Kyrgyzstan lies above Tajikistan; keep those two eastern members of the group
in that order.

17
00:03:09.948 --> 00:03:19.248 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Tajikistan lies below Kyrgyzstan, east of Uzbekistan and north of Afghanistan.
Its mountainous eastern end reaches China.

18
00:03:19.911 --> 00:03:31.428 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Pamirs form a meeting point of great Asian mountain ranges. Tajik National
Park contains high plateaux, glaciers and peaks rising above seven thousand
metres.

19
00:03:32.112 --> 00:03:42.433 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Picture the knot of mountains below Kyrgyzstan. Tajikistan is the southern
mountain country in this pair, with Afghanistan immediately beyond its
southern border.

20
00:03:43.805 --> 00:03:47.601 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Let's bring Afghanistan and its neighbours into view.

21
00:03:57.922 --> 00:04:09.950 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Afghanistan is landlocked, south of the Central Asian republics, east of Iran
and west of Pakistan. Its narrow northeastern Wakhan Corridor reaches China.

22
00:04:10.603 --> 00:04:26.253 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
The Bamiyan Valley preserves Buddhist caves and the niches of two giant
standing Buddhas. The Taliban destroyed those statues in 2001; the surviving
landscape records a much older cultural crossroads.

23
00:04:26.918 --> 00:04:38.052 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Remember Afghanistan below the Central Asian cluster, with a thin finger
reaching China. The empty Buddha niches are its historical hook in the
mountain interior.

24
00:04:49.183 --> 00:05:01.803 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Kazakhstan caps the group. Uzbekistan sits in the central cluster,
Turkmenistan reaches the Caspian on the southwest, and Kyrgyzstan lies above
Tajikistan beside China. Afghanistan is below them.

25
00:05:02.459 --> 00:05:18.458 line:2% position:50%,center size:96% align:center
Recall migrating birds, Samarkand’s observatory, Merv’s oasis, the Manas
storyteller, the Pamir mountain knot and Bamiyan’s empty niches. Trace the
neighbour pattern before trying the names without labels.
